readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|Showing the ''unmeasureableness'' of his Godhed.
{{right|''Fryth'', Bok made by Him (an. 1533). (''Encyc. Dict.'')}}}}

'''unmeasurably†''' (un-mezh′ṳ̄r-a-bli), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''unmesurably''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''measurably''.] Immeasurably.
{{smaller block|The nyght folowynge there rose a wondre grete tempeste of excedynge moche wynde, and therewithall it rayned and hayled so ''vnmesurably'' that no man myght loke forthe aboue the hatches. {{right|''Sir R. Guylforde'', Pylgrymage, p. 72.}}}}

'''unmeasured''' (un-mezh′ṳ̄rd), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not measured; plentiful beyond measure; hence, immense; infinite; boundless.
{{smaller block|The ''unmeasured'' cycles of a limitless future.
{{right|''J. R. Macduff'', Memories of Patmos, p. 16.}}
Peopling, they also, the ''unmeasured'' solitudes of time.
{{right|''Carlyle.''}}}}
'''2.''' Not subject to or obeying any musical rule of measure, time, or rhythm; irregular; capricious.
{{smaller block|The ''unmeasured'' notes of that strange lyre. {{right|''Shelley.''}}}}

'''unmechanize''' (un-mek′a-nīz), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''mechanize''.] To undo or destroy the mechanism of; unmake; destroy; throw out of gear. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Embryotic evils that could ''unmechanize'' thy frame.
{{right|''Sterne'', Tristram Shandy, iii. 167.}}}}

'''unmeddle''' (un-med′l), ''v. i.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''meddle''.] To undo or repair the effects of meddling. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Lord Granville ''unmeddles'' and unmuddles.
{{right|''Higginson'', English Statesmen (1875), p. 167.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
{{smaller block|Whenever he did anything which appeared to her to savour of an ''unmentionable'' place.
{{right|''W. S. Gilbert'', Lost Mr. Blake.}}}}

'''unmentionableness''' (un-men′shọn-a-bl-nes), ''n.'' The character of being unmentionable.

'''unmentionables''' (un-men′shọn-a-blz), ''n. pl.'' Trousers or breeches, as an article of dress not to be mentioned in polite circles; inexpressibles. [Colloq. and humorous.]

'''unmercenary''' (un-mėr′se-nạ̄-ri), ''a.'' Not mercenary; not sordid.
{{smaller block|A generous and ''unmercenary'' principle.
{{right|''Bp. Atterbury'', Sermons, I. i.}}}}

'''unmerchantable''' (un-mėr′chạn-ta-bl), ''a.'' Not merchantable; not of a quality fit for the market; unsalable. ''R. Carew.''
{{smaller block|No lot of Meats shall be considered suitable for delivery on contract if twenty (20) per cent. of it is ''unmerchantable.''
{{right|''New York Produce Exchange Report'', 1888–9, p. 167.}}}}

'''unmercied†''' (un-mėr′sid), ''a.'' [< ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''mercy'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Unmerciful; merciless. ''Drayton'', Miseries of Queen Margaret.

'''unmerciful''' (un-mėr′si-fu̇l), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not merciful; not influenced by mercy; unkind; cruel; inhuman; merciless: of persons or things.
{{smaller block|God never can hear the prayers of an ''unmerciful'' man.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Sermons, I. iv.}}}}
'''2.''' Unconscionable; exorbitant.
{{smaller block|''Unmerciful'' demands. {{right|''Pope.''}}}}

'''Unmerciful Parliament.''' {{smaller|Same as ''merciless Parliament'' (which see, under ''Parliament'').}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|The property of oil being ''unmingleable'' with water.
{{right|''Boyle'', Works, I. 536.}}}}

'''unmingled''' (un-ming′gld), ''a.'' Not mingled; not mixed; unmixed; unalloyed; pure: as, to view some event with ''unmingled'' dread.
{{smaller block|Springs on the tops of high hills are . . . pure and ''unmingled.''
{{right|''Bacon'', Nat. Hist., § 396.}}}}

'''unmiraculous''' (un-mi-rak′ū-lus), ''a.'' Not miraculous. ''Young'', Night Thoughts, ix.

'''unmiraculously''' (un-mi-rak′ū-lus-li), ''adv.'' In an unmiraculous manner; without a miracle.

'''unmiry''' (un-mīr′i), ''a.'' Not miry; not muddy; not foul with dirt. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|With safe ''unmiry'' feet.
{{right|''Gay'', Trivia, iii.}}}}

'''unmistakable''' (un-mis-tā′ka-bl), ''a.'' That cannot be mistaken or misunderstood; clear; evident. Also ''unmistakeable.''
{{smaller block|Not the Scripture, but ''unmistakeable'' and indefectible oral tradition, was the rule of faith.
{{right|''Tillotson.''}}}}
={{smaller|'''Syn.''' Palpable, manifest, obvious, patent, unequivocal, unambiguous, decided.}}

'''unmistakably''' (un-mis-tā′kạ-bli), ''adv.'' In an unmistakable manner; so as not to be mistaken. Also ''unmistakeably.''
{{smaller block|<poem>She went first to the best adviser, God—
Whose finger ''unmistakably'' was felt
In all this retribution of the past.</poem>
{{right|''Browning'', Ring and Book, i. 116.}}}}

'''unmiter, unmitre''' (un-mī′tėr), ''v. t.'' To deprive of a miter; degrade or depose from the rank and dignity of a bishop. ''Milton.'' [Rare.]
